{"name":"Faded Jeans","hex":"#5dbdcb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"faded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That has lost some of its former vividness and colour.","That has lost some of its former strength or vigour."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of fade"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/faded"},{"term":"jeans","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pair of trousers made from denim cotton."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of jean"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jeans"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jeans","blocks":[{"text":"Jeans are a type of trousers made from denim or dungaree cloth. Often the term \"jeans\" refers to a particular style of trousers, called \"blue jeans\", with the addition of copper pocket rivets added by Jacob W. Davis in 1871 and patented by Davis and Levi Strauss on May 20, 1873. Prior to the patent, the term \"blue jeans\" had been long in use for various items of workwear, constructed from a heavy blue-colored denim fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Jeans.jpg/330px-Jeans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeans","wikidata":"Q83363"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Blue_jeans_fading.png/500px-Blue_jeans_fading.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Blue_jeans_fading.png/1280px-Blue_jeans_fading.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Blue_jeans_fading.png/500px-Blue_jeans_fading.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Microscopic image of faded fabric","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_jeans_fading.png"},